<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rebellion of the Seven States</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">154 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Revolt of the Seven Kingdoms, Qi Guo zhi Luan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rebellion of the Seven States was a revolt in 154 BCE by the semi-autonomous regional kings of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> against Emperor Jing, who sought to centralise the government further. On the advice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-cuo">Chao Cuo</a> the emperor had cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> from the principalities of Chu, Zhao, Jiaoxi and Wu; Liu Pi, Prince of Wu, then led seven princes in revolt, demanding Chao's execution. While Wu and Chu besieged Suiyang, the capital of Liang, the imperial commander Zhou Yafu cut their supply lines until their armies starved and collapsed. The rebellion was crushed within three months, and the powers of the princes were gradually reduced under Emperor Jing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-seven-states.jpg" alt="Rebellion of the Seven States" width="330" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rebellion_of_the_Seven_States.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>At the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>, Emperor Gaozu of Han, gave princely titles to many relatives over territories covering roughly a third to a half of the empire, to hold for the Liu family the lands not ruled directly through the commandery system. Under Emperor Wen the princes set their own laws, minted coins with his approval and collected their own taxes, and many ignored the imperial government. When Emperor Jing came to the throne in 157 BCE, the rich principality of Wu, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and salt, was especially domineering.</p><p>Jing also had a personal quarrel with Liu Pi, Prince of Wu. Around 179-175 BCE, as Crown Prince Qi, he had beaten Liu Pi's heir, Liu Xian, to death with a liubo board in an argument over the game. His key advisor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-cuo">Chao Cuo</a>, proposing to cut down the principalities for offences Emperor Wen had ignored, argued that if the princes were going to rebel, it was better they did so early rather than later, when they might be better prepared.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Liu Pi was joined by the princes of Chu, Jiaoxi, Jiaodong, Zichuan, Jinan and Zhao. Qi and Jibei agreed to join but did not, and the southern kingdoms of Donghai and Minyue sent troops, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> promised help and sent none. Liu Pi massed the Wu and Chu armies against Liang, ruled by the emperor's brother Liu Wu, and besieged its capital.</p><p>Zhou Yafu, warned of assassins on his road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, let Liang take the brunt, refused even the emperor's order to relieve it, and sent cavalry to cut the rebels' supply lines. Held off by Liang's generals Han Anguo and Zhang Yu, the Wu and Chu forces turned on Zhou's camp, failed to break it, starved and collapsed. Liu Pi fled to Donghai, which killed him. Elsewhere Luan Bu relieved the Qi capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a>, the capital of Zhao, fell when a levee was broken to flood its walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The princes of Chu and Zhao killed themselves, the four princes who had besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a> were executed or allowed to commit suicide, and their lands were seized by the central government. Had the princes prevailed, the Han would in all likelihood have broken up into a loose confederation of states. Instead the principality system survived, but under Emperor Jing and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> the princes' powers and lands were gradually reduced, and the idea that a unified empire was normal began to settle in.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rebellion of the Seven States” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire was divided into commanderies administered by centrally appointed officials, and after the <b>Rebellion of the Seven States</b> in 154 BCE, Emperors Jing and Wu progressively reduced the autonomous kingdoms to centrally controlled units.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Rebellion of the Seven States</b> in 154 BCE the court cut their powers: from 145 BCE they could appoint no official above 400 bushels and became nominal lords, living on a share of their kingdom’s taxes.</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li><li><blockquote>It took part in the <b>Rebellion of the Seven States</b> in 154 BCE but then killed the rebel king of Wu at Han urging.</blockquote><a href="/state/dongou">Dong'ou</a></li></ul></section>
